Calibration Mode should include a dedicated BPM analysis and BPM entry section that works alongside waveform-based timing alignment. Right now calibration focuses only on offset and start point alignment, but BPM is equally important because it defines how musical time is structured once the chart is synced.\
Inside Calibration Mode, there should be a clearly separated BPM panel that allows both manual BPM entry and automatic BPM detection. This panel is not part of the main waveform interaction area. It is a supporting control section that updates the timing model used by the beat grid and preview playback system.\
Manual BPM entry should be a simple input field where the user can directly type a BPM value. When this value is changed, the system must immediately update the beat grid spacing across the waveform and preview systems. This includes recalculating measure divisions, beat intervals, and timing markers in real time without requiring a reload or recalibration reset. The BPM value becomes part of the active calibration state and directly affects how musical time is interpreted across the entire chart.\
Alongside manual entry, there should be an automatic BPM calibration function that is triggered by holding or pressing the spacebar while in a dedicated BPM detection mode. When activated, the system plays the audio while analyzing rhythmic spacing patterns in the waveform. It detects recurring transient intervals such as kick drums, snare patterns, or consistent beat spacing, and uses this data to estimate the approximate BPM of the song.\
Importantly, during this BPM detection playback, the calibration cursor must remain visually stable in terms of position tracking behavior. The system should continue to show playback progress through the waveform, but it should not modify or shift the calibration marker or offset during detection. The purpose of this mode is purely analytical, not structural. It listens and measures, but does not alter timing alignment while it is running.\
Once detection completes or is manually stopped, the system should present an estimated BPM value to the user. This value should be shown as a suggestion rather than an automatic overwrite. The user must confirm whether they want to apply it to the calibration state. If confirmed, the BPM grid, waveform beat overlay, and playback timing system should update immediately to reflect the new BPM.\
The BPM detection system should be treated as a helper tool rather than an authoritative source. It provides an estimate based on audio rhythm patterns, but the final BPM decision always belongs to the user because rhythm games often include intentional BPM changes, swing timing, or irregular structures that automated detection cannot fully interpret correctly.\
This BPM calibration layer must integrate directly with the existing KSON timing system so that any BPM applied here is stored as part of the chart\'92s metadata and can be used later by the preview engine to correctly compute playback speed, lane movement, and beat synchronization. It ensures that Calibration Mode is not only responsible for aligning the start point of the chart but also for establishing the fundamental tempo structure that the rest of the editor relies on.\
