\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The orthographic preview mode must behave like a strict 2D projection of the Sound Voltex lane system, not a pseudo-3D or perspective-influenced rendering. Right now the issue is that elements such as BT buttons, FX lanes, and lasers are not correctly constrained to a flat plane, causing them to visually \'93stick out\'94 or appear incorrectly layered. In true SDVX orthographic view, everything exists on a single 2D gameplay plane where depth is not visually simulated. The lane itself must move strictly in a linear downward direction with uniform speed mapping, exactly matching beat progression. There must be no perspective convergence, no vanishing point distortion, and no simulated depth stretching. Official SDVX gameplay still uses perspective in gameplay mode, but orthographic/editor style views in tools and references behave as flat timing representations for clarity and accuracy.\
The BT buttons must be fully embedded into the lane plane. They are not floating objects and must not visually protrude or have depth offset. Each BT input should behave like a 2D hit object that only changes state when it intersects the judgment line at the bottom. When active, BT buttons should only visually \'93light up\'94 at the exact moment they cross the judgment line. They should not animate independently in space or appear offset from the lane surface. FX buttons follow the same rule and must remain strictly 2D overlays aligned to their lane positions.\
Lasers (VOL-L and VOL-R) must also be fully flattened into the same 2D orthographic plane during edit and orthographic preview mode. They must not appear as segmented 3D ribbons or floating curves. Instead, they must be rendered as continuous smoothed spline paths with no visible control points, no anchor dots, and no intermediate construction geometry during playback or non-edit states. The system must explicitly hide all pen tool handles, anchor nodes, and intermediate control points unless the laser is actively selected in edit mode. This mirrors professional vector editing behavior.\
To match professional vector workflows, laser editing must emulate Adobe Photoshop Pen Tool mechanics. In Photoshop, the Pen Tool works through anchor points that define structure and B\'e9zier handles that define curvature. When a point is not actively selected, its handles are hidden. When multiple points are connected, Photoshop renders a smooth continuous path without showing internal segmentation. The same behavior must be applied here. Left click places anchor points. Click and drag defines curve tension. Modifier keys must replicate standard vector tool behavior: Ctrl or Command toggles selection versus direct path manipulation, Shift constrains directional flow or enforces smooth curve continuity, and Alt or Option allows independent handle adjustment for sharper curve control. The goal is to make VOL laser editing behave like professional vector path design rather than game-specific input editing.\
Laser rendering must also merge adjacent segments into unified continuous curves. Currently the system appears to be rendering laser sections as independent pieces without proper continuity, which breaks both visual fidelity and performance. In correct SDVX style rendering, a laser is always a single continuous interpolated curve even if it was created from multiple editing points. Any segmentation should exist only in the editor data model, not in the render output.\
A major optimization issue is caused by lasers being recalculated and redrawn too frequently. The system must separate laser edit representation from runtime representation. During editing, full vector control data is visible and interactive. During playback or orthographic preview without active selection, lasers must be precomputed into optimized spline meshes and cached so that rendering does not recompute geometry every frame. This significantly reduces lag, especially on dense charts.\
The orthographic lane itself must enforce strict linear downward movement of all gameplay elements. Notes, lasers, FX objects, and BT objects must all move along a uniform vertical axis tied directly to musical time progression. There must be no easing, no acceleration curves, and no artificial perspective scaling in orthographic mode. Movement speed is strictly determined by BPM and timing data, not visual interpolation.\
Performance optimization must also include lane-level culling. Not all lanes or objects should be rendered simultaneously at full fidelity. Only objects within or near the active judgment window should be fully rendered. Objects outside the interaction range should be either simplified or excluded entirely from rendering calculations. This reduces unnecessary GPU load and prevents frame drops caused by overdraw of offscreen elements.\
Additionally, the system must correct a specific visual issue where lasers appear incorrectly terminated or extended. Each laser must begin exactly at the player interaction origin point and end exactly at its defined endpoint in chart data. The tail of the laser must reflect its actual end position, particularly for slam transitions. There must be no visual extrapolation beyond defined endpoints. Any smoothing applied must respect endpoint anchoring and must not extend geometry beyond the defined curve limits.\
Finally, orthographic preview must strictly remain a 2D timing visualization system. It is not a degraded version of gameplay, but a precise alignment tool for chart editing and verification. All gameplay logic remains intact, but all spatial representation must be flattened into a consistent 2D plane with strict linear timing flow, fully hidden control geometry, and vector-style laser editing behavior modeled after professional design tools like Photoshop Pen Tool workflows.\
